 🥳 Happy Friday! Today's newsletter, edited by Alex Fitzpatrick, is 538 words, a 2-min. read. Thanks to Amy Stern for copy editing . 

 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 1 big thing: Anthropic slumps amid tech sell-off 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Illustration: Brendan Lynch/Axios Anthropic shares are slumping in their first full trading day, while the S&amp;P 500 flirts with correction territory, Axios' Nathan Bomey reports. 

 The Claude maker was seen as a catalyst that could prop up the AI economy. But its record-setting IPO arrived just as investors were losing faith in the emerging tech and the huge spending supporting it. By the numbers: Anthropic shares were down about 10% as of midafternoon, trading at about $87.

 That's far below yesterday's opening price ($111.50) and their all-time high ($115.10) . 🖼️ The big picture: Anthropic's downturn coincides with a significant drop-off for chips supplier Nvidia, which has now lost more than $1.6 trillion in market cap from its all-time high.

 Nvidia is grappling with the fallout from the Jensen Huang scandal , plus fears over unsustainable AI spending. Other AI-linked stocks are also struggling, with the Nasdaq down 4.3% as of midafternoon.

 Tech analyst Dan Ives wrote: "This is a painful tech sell-off." 👀 What we're watching: The broader S&amp;P 500 was down about 2.8%.

 Closing there would leave it more than 10% below its 52-week high — the textbook definition of a market correction. Go deeper . 🧮 Meanwhile: Anthropic mathematician Levent Alpöge claims to have solved math's most infamous unsolved problem — with Claude's help.

 The Wall Street Journal reports: "If the proof checks out, it would count as the most remarkable accomplishment in the year of AI's extraordinary math progress." Go deeper (gift link) . 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 2. 📈 Consumer sentiment surges 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Data: University of Michigan; Chart: Neil Irwin/Axios Consumer sentiment popped this month amid the midterm elections and a moderation in energy prices, Axios' Neil Irwin reports. 

 🖼️ The big picture: Sentiment remains below its pre-Iran war levels, but Americans feel better about their personal finances.

 They also view the soon-to-be divided government as better for their pocketbooks. 📈 By the numbers: The University of Michigan's overall consumer sentiment index was 51.5 in November, up from 48 in October.

 That 7.3% rise was fueled by a strong gain in expectations for the future. Go deeper . 

 
 
